1
00:00:04,470 --> 00:00:06,320
Hello, hello, K S Lo students!

2
00:00:06,370 --> 00:00:09,200
It’s me, Kayla, your grammar study buddy!

3
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:14,200
Grammar can be tricky, but don’t worry.
We’ll break it down together, step by step.

4
00:00:14,250 --> 00:00:17,000
Watch carefully, because
there’s a quick quiz at the end.

5
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:17,740
Ready?

6
00:00:17,790 --> 00:00:19,503
Let’s level up!

7
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:25,030
Look! Is, read, was, reaches, gives…

8
00:00:25,210 --> 00:00:27,770
Present, past, then present again!

9
00:00:27,910 --> 00:00:28,960
Is that allowed?

10
00:00:29,110 --> 00:00:30,160
Let’s find out!

11
00:00:31,083 --> 00:00:32,683
Here’s our Tense Map.

12
00:00:33,003 --> 00:00:36,583
Yellow is simple: facts,
habits and finished actions.

13
00:00:36,683 --> 00:00:39,703
Pink is continuous: actions in progress.

14
00:00:40,083 --> 00:00:41,543
Let’s unlock them!

15
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:44,310
Level one: the present!

16
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:48,470
The simple present uses the basic verb.

17
00:00:48,550 --> 00:00:50,850
With he, she or it, add an s.

18
00:00:50,970 --> 00:00:52,020
She plays!

19
00:00:53,017 --> 00:00:57,337
Use it for facts. Water freezes
at zero degrees Celsius.

20
00:00:57,517 --> 00:01:00,157
Things that are true
now. I live in Tsing Yi.

21
00:01:00,757 --> 00:01:04,517
And habits. Richard wakes
up at 7 a.m. every day.

22
00:01:08,483 --> 00:01:12,433
The present continuous:
am, is or are, plus -ing.

23
00:01:12,483 --> 00:01:14,863
It has five uses. Give me five!

24
00:01:14,983 --> 00:01:18,113
One: right now. Look! Snow is falling.

25
00:01:18,163 --> 00:01:22,433
Two: temporary situations. I
am learning to swim at present.

26
00:01:22,483 --> 00:01:26,553
Three: changing trends. House
husbands are becoming more popular.

27
00:01:26,603 --> 00:01:30,733
Four: photos. Everyone is
smiling happily in the photo.

28
00:01:30,783 --> 00:01:36,363
Five: annoying habits, with always. He
is always complaining about his job!

29
00:01:40,383 --> 00:01:42,303
Level two: the past!

30
00:01:43,817 --> 00:01:46,797
The simple past is for finished actions.

31
00:01:47,057 --> 00:01:50,377
Most verbs add -ed,
but some are irregular.

32
00:01:50,537 --> 00:01:52,397
I was born in 2013.

33
00:01:52,637 --> 00:01:55,117
A fire broke out in my building yesterday.

34
00:01:57,583 --> 00:02:01,173
The past continuous:
was or were, plus -ing.

35
00:02:01,223 --> 00:02:05,073
Use it for an action in
progress at a past time.

36
00:02:05,123 --> 00:02:08,303
We were having a barbecue
at four o’clock yesterday.

37
00:02:08,603 --> 00:02:12,013
For a long action
interrupted by a short one.

38
00:02:12,063 --> 00:02:15,363
She was waiting for the bus
when it suddenly rained.

39
00:02:16,763 --> 00:02:19,273
And for two actions at the same time.

40
00:02:19,323 --> 00:02:23,283
Fred was watching TV while
his mum was preparing dinner.

41
00:02:27,417 --> 00:02:28,200
Nice one!

42
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:30,439
Now, let’s move on to the next part!

43
00:02:30,883 --> 00:02:33,963
Level three: non-action verbs!

44
00:02:35,217 --> 00:02:39,817
Non-action verbs describe
feelings, opinions or states.

45
00:02:40,117 --> 00:02:42,507
We don’t usually use them with -ing.

46
00:02:42,557 --> 00:02:48,857
Like, want, think, know,
see, smell and have.

47
00:02:52,883 --> 00:02:57,623
I think game shows are
exciting. Not I am thinking!

48
00:02:57,883 --> 00:03:01,903
Mike has one brother and one
sister. Not Mike is having!

49
00:03:04,117 --> 00:03:05,317
Plot twist!

50
00:03:05,557 --> 00:03:08,437
Some of these verbs can take
-ing when they’re actions.

51
00:03:08,677 --> 00:03:11,837
What is she thinking now?
That’s the action of thinking.

52
00:03:12,297 --> 00:03:15,117
We were having dinner at
a Japanese restaurant.

53
00:03:15,317 --> 00:03:17,097
Here, having means eating!

54
00:03:19,317 --> 00:03:22,177
Level four: tenses in stories!

55
00:03:23,650 --> 00:03:27,490
Stories that happened in the
past usually use past tenses.

56
00:03:28,030 --> 00:03:33,570
Once upon a time, there was a beautiful
princess who had a kind heart…

57
00:03:35,517 --> 00:03:40,007
But in a synopsis, a short
summary of a film, play or book,

58
00:03:40,057 --> 00:03:44,417
or when we retell a story,
we often use present tenses.

59
00:03:44,677 --> 00:03:46,017
It’s more dramatic!

60
00:03:46,197 --> 00:03:50,957
Dora and Sam are resting on the beach
when suddenly, they spot a shark!

61
00:03:52,850 --> 00:03:56,250
Careful! Real-world
facts stay in the past.

62
00:03:56,390 --> 00:03:59,330
The film was a big hit
when it came out in 2008.

63
00:03:59,470 --> 00:04:03,170
In the film, Harry the superhero
saves thousands of lives.

64
00:04:05,117 --> 00:04:06,467
Back to Snow White!

65
00:04:06,517 --> 00:04:10,277
Is: true now. Read and was: finished.

66
00:04:10,557 --> 00:04:15,357
Reaches, gives, saves and live:
the story, told in the present.

67
00:04:15,437 --> 00:04:17,297
Every tense has a reason!

68
00:04:18,517 --> 00:04:21,517
Boss quiz! Say your answers out loud!

69
00:04:25,833 --> 00:04:29,313
Is sleeping! It’s happening right now.

70
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:37,700
Was doing! A long action, interrupted.

71
00:04:42,833 --> 00:04:46,553
Want! It’s a non-action verb, so no -ing.

72
00:04:51,633 --> 00:04:55,073
Flies! In a synopsis, we use the present.

73
00:04:56,517 --> 00:04:57,747
Here’s your cheat sheet.

74
00:04:57,797 --> 00:04:59,637
Pause the video and take a screenshot!

75
00:05:04,183 --> 00:05:06,520
Great work today, K S Lo students!

76
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:10,863
Keep practising, and try using this
grammar in your own writing and speaking.

77
00:05:10,913 --> 00:05:12,355
See you next time! Bye-bye!

